    "q-168892": {
      "dom_id": "question-168892",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Classify the critical points using the second partial derivatives test.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima since $\\,H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0\\,\\,$ and $\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $\\,\\,H(0,0)$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0$",
      "worked_solution": "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nIn part 4, we found the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$ Their corresponding Hessian determinants and the second partial derivatives at these points were computed in part 6.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify our critical points:\n\n- $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima of $f$ since $H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=1\\,408>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)=32>0.$\n\n- $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $H(0,0)=-192<0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
